## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model simple state transitions and undo/redo semantics with basic data structures.

## Key Concepts

- History management with stacks
- Amortized O(1) operations
- Edge cases: empty history, initial page

## Code Example

```javascript
class BrowserHistory {
  constructor(startUrl) {
    this.back = [];
    this.forward = [];
    this.currentUrl = startUrl ?? null;
  }
  visit(url) {
    if (this.currentUrl !== null) this.back.push(this.currentUrl);
    this.forward = [];
    this.currentUrl = url;
  }
  back(steps) {
    while (steps > 0 && this.back.length) {
      this.forward.push(this.currentUrl);
      this.currentUrl = this.back.pop();
      steps--;
    }
    return this.currentUrl;
  }
  forward(steps) {
    while (steps > 0 && this.forward.length) {
      this.back.push(this.currentUrl);
      this.currentUrl = this.forward.pop();
      steps--;
    }
    return this.currentUrl;
  }
  current() {
    return this.currentUrl;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale to multiple tabs with independent histories?
- How would you extend to persist history across sessions without refetching pages?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement a compact prefix-based dictionary with duplicates and fast prefix queries using a Trie.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node counts; handling duplicates via passCount and endCount.
- Insert/erase adjust counts; erase stops if word not present.
- Prefix query is O(length(prefix)).

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode{ constructor(){ this.next=new Array(26).fill(null); this.pass=0; this.end=0; }}
class Trie{ constructor(){ this.root=new TrieNode(); }
 insert(w){ let p=this.root; for(const ch of w){ const i=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if(!p.next[i]) p.next[i]=new TrieNode(); p=p.next[i]; p.pass++; } p.end++; }
 erase(w){ if(!this.contains(w)) return; let p=this.root; for(const ch of w){ const i=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; p=p.next[i]; p.pass--; } p.end--; }
 countPrefix(pfx){ let p=this.root; for(const ch of pfx){ const i=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; p=p.next[i]; if(!p) return 0; } return p.pass; }
 contains(w){ let p=this.root; for(const ch of w){ const i=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; p=p.next[i]; if(!p) return false; } return p.end>0; }
 }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for Unicode inputs or dynamic alphabets?
- What are the memory implications for large vocabularies with many prefixes?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests advanced spatial indexing with dynamic updates and order statistics in 3D, relevant for large-scale simulations (NVIDIA) or online marketplaces with spatial constraints (Instacart, Amazon).

## Key Concepts
- 3D range trees and nested segment trees
- Weight compression and Fenwick trees for counts/sums
- Order statistics via binary search over weight indices
- Handling duplicates via multiplicities per weight index
- Fractional cascading for faster multi-node queries

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton: define core structures and update/query stubs
class Fenwick {
  constructor(size) {
    this.size = size;
    this.tree = new Array(size + 1).fill(0);
  }

  update(index, delta) {
    for (let i = index; i <= this.size; i += i & -i) {
      this.tree[i] += delta;
    }
  }

  query(index) {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let i = index; i > 0; i -= i & -i) {
      sum += this.tree[i];
    }
    return sum;
  }
}

